---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudy from '../../components/CaseStudy.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Work', url: '/work/' },
  { name: 'COVID-19 Sentiments', url: '/work/covid19-sentiments/' },
];

const technologies = [
  'Python',
  'Natural Language Processing',
  'Sentiment Analysis',
  'Machine Learning',
  'Data Visualization',
  'Social Media Analytics',
];
---
<Base
  title="COVID-19 Sentiment Analysis"
  description="Machine learning system for classifying sentiment in COVID-19 related tweets, analyzing public sentiment trends during the pandemic."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  articleDate="2021-04-15"
>
  <main class="profile">
    <Crumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Work', href: '/work/' }, { label: 'COVID-19 sentiment' }]} />
    <CaseStudy
      title="COVID-19 Sentiment Analysis"
      category="Machine Learning · NLP"
      description="A sentiment classification system analyzing COVID-19 related tweets to understand public sentiment patterns and trends during the pandemic."
      githubUrl="https://github.com/likarajo/covid19Sentiments"
      externalUrl="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/covid-19-sentiment-analysis-rajarshi-chattopadhyay/"
      technologies={technologies}
    >
      <section>
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>
          This project analyzes sentiment in COVID-19 related tweets to understand public emotional
          responses during the pandemic. Using natural language processing and machine learning,
          the system classifies tweets as positive, negative, or neutral, revealing patterns in
          how people expressed concerns, fears, and hopes during this unprecedented global event.
        </p>
        <p>
          Understanding public sentiment during a public health crisis provides valuable insights
          for policymakers, health organizations, and researchers tracking the social and
          psychological impacts of the pandemic.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Challenge</h2>
        <p>
          Sentiment analysis on social media data presents unique challenges:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Informal Language:</strong> Tweets contain slang, abbreviations, emojis,
            and non-standard grammar
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Context Sensitivity:</strong> Same words can have different sentiment in
            different contexts (e.g., "sick" as slang vs. illness)
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Sarcasm and Irony:</strong> Difficult to detect without understanding context
            and tone
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Evolving Vocabulary:</strong> New terms and hashtags emerge rapidly during
            major events
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Class Imbalance:</strong> Negative tweets may dominate during crisis periods
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Solution Approach</h2>

        <h3>Data Collection and Preprocessing</h3>
        <p>
          The project processes tweet data through several stages:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Collection:</strong> Gather COVID-19 related tweets using pandemic-related
            keywords and hashtags
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cleaning:</strong> Remove URLs, mentions, special characters, and normalize text
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Tokenization:</strong> Break text into individual tokens while preserving
            relevant emojis
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Feature Extraction:</strong> Convert text to numerical representations for
            machine learning
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Sentiment Classification</h3>
        <p>
          The system employs machine learning models to classify sentiment:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Text vectorization using TF-IDF or word embeddings</li>
          <li>Classification models (e.g., Naive Bayes, Logistic Regression, or Neural Networks)</li>
          <li>Multi-class classification: Positive, Negative, Neutral</li>
          <li>Model evaluation using accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Trend Analysis</h3>
        <p>
          Beyond individual tweet classification, the project analyzes sentiment trends:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Temporal patterns: How sentiment changed over time</li>
          <li>Topic-based sentiment: Different attitudes toward vaccines, lockdowns, cases, etc.</li>
          <li>Geographic patterns: Regional differences in sentiment (when location data available)</li>
          <li>Correlation with events: Spikes in negative/positive sentiment around major announcements</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Technical Implementation</h2>

        <h3>Feature Engineering</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Text Features:</strong> TF-IDF vectors, n-grams, word embeddings
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Metadata Features:</strong> Tweet length, hashtag count, mention count
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Emoji Analysis:</strong> Sentiment signals from emoji usage
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Domain-Specific Terms:</strong> COVID-related vocabulary (vaccine, mask,
            quarantine, etc.)
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Model Pipeline</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>Data preprocessing and cleaning</li>
          <li>Feature extraction and vectorization</li>
          <li>Train-test split with stratification</li>
          <li>Model training and hyperparameter tuning</li>
          <li>Evaluation and error analysis</li>
          <li>Deployment for batch or real-time classification</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Insights and Findings</h2>

        <div class="highlight-box">
          <h3>Key Observations</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Emotional Trajectory:</strong> Sentiment patterns reflected the stages of
              the pandemic—initial fear, adaptation, vaccine hope, and fatigue
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Topic Variation:</strong> Different aspects of COVID-19 (health measures,
              economic impact, vaccines) elicited distinct sentiment patterns
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Information Spread:</strong> Sentiment often correlated with major news
              events and policy announcements
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          The analysis revealed how public discourse evolved as the pandemic progressed, with
          sentiment shifting from uncertainty and concern toward more nuanced discussions of
          policy, science, and long-term impacts.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Applications</h2>
        <p>
          This sentiment analysis approach is applicable to various domains:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Public Health:</strong> Monitor public response to health campaigns and policies
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Crisis Communication:</strong> Track sentiment during emergencies and disasters
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Social Research:</strong> Study collective emotional responses to major events
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Misinformation Detection:</strong> Identify patterns in sentiment around
            false information
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Social media sentiment analysis provides real-time insights into public opinion
            during crises
          </li>
          <li>
            Preprocessing and feature engineering are critical for handling informal social
            media text
          </li>
          <li>
            Temporal analysis reveals how sentiment evolves in response to events and policies
          </li>
          <li>
            Context matters: Same words carry different sentiment depending on the situation
          </li>
          <li>
            Combining automated sentiment classification with human validation improves
            accuracy and insights
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Future Directions</h2>
        <p>
          Potential extensions to this work:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Multi-lingual sentiment analysis for global pandemic response tracking</li>
          <li>Fine-grained emotion classification beyond positive/negative/neutral</li>
          <li>Integration with epidemiological data to correlate sentiment with case trends</li>
          <li>Real-time dashboard for monitoring ongoing sentiment patterns</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <p class="page-end">
        <a href="/work/">Back to all projects</a>
        {" · "}
        <a href="https://github.com/likarajo/covid19Sentiments" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub</a>
        {" · "}
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/covid-19-sentiment-analysis-rajarshi-chattopadhyay/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Article</a>
      </p>
    </CaseStudy>
  </main>
</Base>